## 1. Purpose and Boundary

The selected q79 topological duality packets already transport the hidden
source character to a rank-nine determinant-one `P(3,9)` class. The explicit
holomorphic Hartshorne-Serre representative, however, fails the ordinary
degree-zero BHT `WIT1` test along the whole support curve `E_B`. This left an
apparent gap between the topological two-circle transform and the
holomorphic fiberwise transform.

This theorem inserts the locked gerbe curvings into that comparison. It proves
three things:

1. exactly one of the two integrable target fiber complex orientations makes
   the Poincare bimodule curvature of type `(1,1)`;
2. in that orientation the order-three source curving changes the local
   twisted fiber pair from `(3,0)` to `(3,9)`, which Fourier-Mukai transports
   to the already selected target twisted pair `(9,-3)`;
3. for the explicit Hartshorne-Serre representative, the corrected local
   transform is a rank-nine vector bundle away from exactly two base fibers.

The remaining defect on those two fibers is one shifted skyscraper component
per fiber. Global holomorphic gerbe descent, removal of that defect, physical
local freeness, stability and HYM are not proved here. In particular,
`B.HS.01` and `UST.G3` remain open.

## 2. Unique Holomorphic Fiber Orientation

On the source elliptic fiber use the locked complex form

\[
\zeta=s+i a.
\]

The target q79 circle pair admits two integrable fiber forms

\[
\eta_\epsilon=\theta+i\epsilon t,
\qquad \epsilon\in\{+1,-1\},
\]

because `dtheta=3 delta`, `dt=0`, and `delta` is of type `(1,1)`.
The Poincare bimodule curvature is

\[
F=-\theta\wedge a-t\wedge s.
\]

In the complex coframe `(eta_epsilon,zeta)`, its four type coefficients,
written as multiples of `i`, are

\[
\begin{array}{c|cccc}
 & \eta\zeta & \eta\bar\zeta & \bar\eta\zeta &
 \bar\eta\bar\zeta\\
\hline
F/i & (1+\epsilon)/4 & (-1+\epsilon)/4 &
      (1-\epsilon)/4 & (-1-\epsilon)/4 .
\end{array}
\]

For `epsilon=+1`, `F` is nonzero in types `(2,0)` and `(0,2)`. For
`epsilon=-1`, those components vanish and

\[
F=\frac{i}{2}
  \left(\bar\eta\wedge\zeta-\eta\wedge\bar\zeta\right)
\]

is pure `(1,1)`. Thus the holomorphic Poincare condition uniquely selects

\[
\eta=\theta-i t
\]

among the two fiber orientations. This is a relative complex-orientation
selector. It is not a theorem selecting a causal arrow or physical time.

The locked curvings are compatible with the same choice:

\[
B_X=3s\wedge a=\frac{3i}{2}\zeta\wedge\bar\zeta,
\qquad
B_Y=\frac13\theta\wedge t
=-\frac{i}{6}\eta\wedge\bar\eta.
\]

Both are pure `(1,1)`. They are gerbe curvings with nonzero exterior
derivatives, not Chern forms of ordinary global line bundles.

## 3. The Exact Twisted Fiber Pair

Use the locked topological orientation

\[
\int_{E_X}a\wedge s=1.
\]

Then `int B_X=-3`. The ordinary Hartshorne-Serre source has rank three and
fiber degree zero. Its twisted character is

\[
\operatorname{Ch}_{H_X}(\kappa)
=e^{-B_X}\operatorname{ch}(\kappa),
\]

so its fiber degree is

\[
d_X^{\rm tw}=0-3(-3)=9.
\]

Equivalently, on a holomorphic gerbe chart, the local trivializing factor has
degree `+3` per source rank. Denote its restriction by `N`, with
`deg(N)=3`. This notation does not assert that `N` descends to an ordinary
global line bundle on the nontrivial principal elliptic bundle.

The normalized elliptic Fourier-Mukai action is

\[
(r,d)\longmapsto(d,-r).
\]

It therefore sends

\[
(3,9)\longmapsto(9,-3).
\]

This is exactly the locked target twisted row `9-3 theta wedge t+U_Y`.
Multiplication by `exp(B_Y)` contributes `+3 theta wedge t` at rank nine,
so the ordinary target has `(rank,c1_fiber)=(9,0)`. The corrected
holomorphic fiber calculation and the already proved two-circle Chern
transport therefore agree without a fitted parameter.

## 4. Corrected Hartshorne-Serre Fiber Atlas

Let `L=O_E(3p0)`. The locked ordinary restrictions of `kappa_hol` are

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{base stratum} & \kappa|_E\\
\hline
X\setminus E_B & O_E^{\oplus3}\\
E_B,\ a\ne0 & L\oplus Q^\vee\\
E_B,\ a=0 & L\oplus O_E\oplus L^{-1},
\end{array}
\]

where the evaluation sequence is

\[
0\longrightarrow L^{-1}\longrightarrow O_E^{\oplus3}
\longrightarrow Q\longrightarrow0,
\qquad (\operatorname{rk}Q,\deg Q)=(2,3).
\]

After applying the local degree-three gerbe factor `N`, the rows become

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
\text{base stratum} & (\kappa\otimes N)|_E\\
\hline
X\setminus E_B & N^{\oplus3}\\
E_B,\ a\ne0 & (L\otimes N)\oplus(Q^\vee\otimes N)\\
E_B,\ a=0 & (L\otimes N)\oplus N\oplus(L^{-1}\otimes N).
\end{array}
\]

The evaluation kernel `Q^dual` is stable. An elementary proof is available
in this degree: it is a rank-two degree-minus-three subbundle of `O_E^3`.
A degree-zero line subbundle would give a nonzero global section, but the
three evaluation sections form a basis. A degree-minus-one line subbundle
would force all three component maps to be multiples of its unique section
and hence a constant linear relation among that same basis. Both are
impossible. Every line subbundle consequently has degree at most minus two,
which is strict stability.

Tensoring by `N` preserves stability and gives

\[
\deg(Q^\vee\otimes N)=-3+2\cdot3=3>0.
\]

Every positive-degree factor is `WIT0` on an elliptic curve. Hence:

- off `E_B`, the corrected transform is locally free of rank `9`;
- at a generic point of `E_B`, the degree-six line contributes rank `6`
  and the positive stable rank-two factor contributes rank `3`, again giving
  a locally free rank-nine transform;
- at a zero of `a`, the first two positive lines still contribute rank
  `6+3=9`, while `L^{-1} tensor N` has degree zero and transforms to one
  shifted skyscraper object.

The selected degree-two section `a` has exactly two listed zeros. Therefore
the former derived/WIT defect supported along all of `E_B` is reduced, in the
B-field-corrected holomorphic atlas, to exactly two base fibers.

## 5. What Remains Constructive

The local calculation identifies a finite next problem. One must construct a
qutrit-equivariant replacement or deformation of the Hartshorne-Serre map for
which the special degree-zero summand never appears, while preserving:

1. the character-theoretic Hartshorne-Serre obstruction cancellation;
2. the source Chern character `3-H-3u`;
3. the order-three gerbe and full projective descent;
4. the transported hidden rows `(rank,c1,e2',e3')=(9,0,-U_Y,0)` and the
   selected zero torsion lift.

A natural candidate is a basepoint-free qutrit coefficient system replacing
the single degree-two coefficient `a`. This suggestion is not yet a theorem:
the extension class, Chern rows and equivariance must all be recomputed.

**Downstream resolution.** `UST.G3G` supersedes that tentative candidate.
It proves that an ordinary scalar replacement cannot work because the
determinant line has degree two, and instead constructs the exact
qutrit-covariant stable extension that removes the shifted skyscraper on each
special fiber. The remaining issue is the global BHT-alpha/finite-sigma
overlap cocycle, not the local WIT0 repair.

Once that local repair is equipped with the required global overlaps,
relative `WIT0` and base change would provide a global locally free twisted
rank-nine output. It would still need determinant/descent identification at
the holomorphic tier, a certified fixed-metric slope bound, the G3D
common-chamber packet and the physical anomaly/Bianchi checks.

**Downstream resolution.** `UST.G3R` constructs those global overlaps,
preserves the alpha/sigma descent, proves uniform WIT0 and obtains the locally
free tau-projective rank-nine output on `Y` after the alpha-twisted BHT
intermediate. The determinant reduction, target
stability/HYM and anomaly/Bianchi clauses remain open.

